  <!-- ETP-4760 (2026-08-04): KEEP this space-separated ddlutils shape here in the SOURCE file.
       This file has TWO independent consumers: (1) org.openbravo.ddlutils.task.ImportSampledata
       (Ant "import.sample.data", the install-time seeder that runs during smartbuild/
       update.database) reads it via org.apache.ddlutils.io.converters.TimestampConverter, which
       calls java.sql.Timestamp.valueOf(), which is STRICT and requires exactly
       "yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss[.f...]" and REJECTS a "T"/"Z" ISO shape; (2) the runtime
       com.etendoerp.go onboarding importer
       (OnboardingDatasetNormalizer -> DataImportService -> DatetimeDomainType, since DATEFROM is
       AD_Reference_ID=16 "DateTime") requires the OPPOSITE: only the strict
       "yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.S'Z'" shape, no space-separated fallback. No single literal
       satisfies both (verified empirically both ways). The date itself (2025-12-31, safely before
       any real tenant's creation date) is intentional: it backdates the rule so it covers a fresh
       tenant's entire history from day one, mirroring the corrective R20 data-fix's own
       datefrom = AD_Client.created choice. The FORMAT conflict is resolved in CODE, not data: see
       OnboardingDatasetNormalizer's ddlutils-to-ISO reformatting pass (only applied to the
       runtime-only in-memory copy, never to this file) and
       docs/etendo-ad/tenant-remediation-knowledge.md for the full writeup. Do NOT change this
       value's format back to "T"/"Z"; that breaks ImportSampledata instead. -->
  <DATEFROM><![CDATA[2025-12-31 03:00:00.0]]></DATEFROM>
